<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Battle of Panipat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1761">1761</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third Battle of Panipat was fought in 1761 near Panipat in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> and the invading army of the Afghan ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> and his allies. It was one of the largest battles of the eighteenth century and ended in a crushing Maratha defeat with very heavy losses. Accounts of the Maratha Empire treat it as a turning point that halted Maratha expansion in the north.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat.jpg" alt="Third Battle of Panipat" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Third_battle_of_Panipat_13_January_1761.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> gained control of a considerable part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> between 1712 and 1757. In 1758, after an alliance with Adina Beg Khan, they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and drove out Timur Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a>, the son and viceroy of the Afghan ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a>, extending their empire north of the Sindhu river.</p><p>Muslim intellectuals such as Shah Waliullah, alarmed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> advance, appealed to Ahmad Shah. By the end of 1759 he had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and he won to his side the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> under Najib-ud-Daula and the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a>. The Maratha army was led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Sadashivrao Bhau</a>, while the bulk of the Maratha forces stayed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Skirmishes around Karnal and Kunjpura preceded the battle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> stormed Kunjpura and killed or enslaved its Afghan garrison, and Ahmad Shah crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> between 23 and 25 October 1760. After two months of siege and skirmishing, with supplies running out and the Maratha chiefs pressing Bhau to fight, the Marathas left their camp before dawn on 13 January and met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> around 8 a.m. Their line ran some 12 km, with artillery in front, and behind it stood civilians, many on pilgrimage.</p><p>On 14 January 1761 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> left under Ibrahim Khan Gardi broke the first Rohilla attack, and by noon Bhau seemed close to victory. Ahmad Shah then brought up 15,000 reserve troops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a>-mounted swivel guns. When Vishwasrao disappeared in the fighting, Bhau came down from his elephant to lead the battle, and Afghan prisoners taken at Kunjpura revolted inside the Maratha lines. Troops in the vanguard, seeing the general gone and chaos behind them, panicked and scattered. Historians believe 60,000 to 70,000 troops were killed in the battle, though losses are disputed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> were hunted down. According to the bakhar of Kashi Raja, about 40,000 Maratha prisoners were killed the day after the battle, and some 22,000 women and children were driven off as slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia">Jassa Singh Ahluwalia</a> saved about 2,000 captives.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaji-baji-rao">Balaji Baji Rao</a>, was crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> with a relief force when he heard of the defeat; he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> and never recovered from the shock. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> never fully recovered but remained the largest empire on the subcontinent and retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> ten years later; their claim over all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-maratha-wars">Anglo-Maratha Wars</a> of the early nineteenth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Third Battle of Panipat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By 1759 his army had reached Lahore, and on 14 January 1761 it decisively defeated the Marathas under Sadashivrao Bhau at the <b>Third Battle of Panipat</b>, fought along a twelve-kilometre front between largely Muslim and largely Hindu armies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Conflict with the Durrani Empire of Ahmad Shah Abdali over the Punjab and northern India ended in the catastrophic defeat at the <b>Third Battle of Panipat</b> in 1761, which inflicted heavy military and human losses and disrupted revenue systems.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Maratha defeat at <b>Panipat</b> in 1761 his son Mahadji Scindia rebuilt the family's power with a European-trained army and from 1771 dominated the Mughal emperor in Delhi.</blockquote><a href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rohillas supported Ahmad Shah Durrani at the <b>Third Battle of Panipat</b> in 1761.</blockquote><a href="/state/rohilla-state">Rohilla State</a></li></ul></section>
